A static array inside a function is given storage twice
=======================================================

	f()
	{
		static char buf[512];
		...
	}

buf is correctly placed in data, and it correctly persists across
calls - the semantics are right.  But its size is *also* added to the
function's stack frame, and those bytes are reserved on every call and
never touched.

A static scalar does not have this problem.  Only arrays.


Reproducing it
--------------

	counter()
	{
		static int n;
		return ++n;
	}

	char *
	tag()
	{
		static char buf[8];
		buf[0] = 'A';
		buf[1] = 0;
		return buf;
	}

	ccc -m micronix -s st.c

gives

	; FUNC _counter:s
	; params=0 locals=1 frame=2		<- just the temp: correct
	_counter::
		call	fentn
		.dw	-2

	; FUNC _tag:s
	; params=0 locals=1 frame=12		<- 2 for the temp, 8 for buf
	_tag::
		call	fenter
		ld	hl,-12
		add	hl,sp
		ld	sp,hl

and both statics are in data, as they should be:

	S1:	.ds	2			; counter's n
	S3:	.ds	8			; tag's buf

So tag() takes eight bytes off the stack on entry for an array that is
not on the stack.  counter() takes none for its scalar.


What is right about it
----------------------

Worth saying plainly, because it is easy to read the frame size and
conclude the array is on the stack.  It is not.  Running the program
above prints

	scalar static: 1
	scalar static: 2
	scalar static: 3
	array static: same address twice? 1 (3801 vs 3801)

The scalar counts across calls, and the array is at the same address
every time - 0x3801, which is data, not stack.  Nothing observable
about the program is wrong.  The only cost is stack that is reserved
and wasted.


Where it bites
--------------

wsld's copy_segment declares

	static unsigned char cbuf[WINSZ];	/* WINSZ is 512 */

which was made static precisely so that a 512-byte buffer would not be
on the frame of a function called once per segment.  The generated code
is

	_copy_segment::
		call	fentbx
		.dw	-6
		ld	hl,-512
		add	hl,sp
		ld	sp,hl

	; FUNC _copy_segment:v
	; params=6 locals=4 frame=522

522 is the ten bytes of real locals plus the 512 the static did not
need.  The buffer is in data; the stack space is pure loss.

On this machine that matters more than it sounds.  The deepest call
chain in wsld is four frames and the largest real frame is findlib's
1032 bytes, so nothing is close to overflowing today - but the whole
64K is text, data, heap and stack together, and half a kilobyte of
stack reserved for nothing is half a kilobyte the heap cannot have.
The waste scales with the size of the array, which is exactly backwards:
the bigger the buffer, the more reason there was to make it static, and
the more stack the declaration costs.

It also makes frame sizes lie.  Reading "frame=522" and concluding that
copy_segment puts half a kilobyte on the stack is the obvious reading
and the wrong one, and frame sizes are what you go looking at when a
program on a small machine runs out of room.


Not fixed
---------

Documented deliberately, not fixed.  Nothing is incorrect at runtime,
the workaround is to move the array to file scope if the stack matters,
and the fix belongs in pass1's frame accounting rather than anywhere it
would be reached from here.

The fix, when it happens: whatever decides a local needs frame space
has to skip a declaration that has been given static storage.  The
scalar path already does this - counter()'s n is not in its frame - so
the two paths have diverged, and the array path is the one that did not
get the memo.
